# `rust-analyzer scip .` output for this repository: a build artifact, 2.6 MB,
# regenerated in ~14s. Root-anchored on purpose — `tests/fixtures/rust/index.scip`
# is committed so the tier-B tests run without rust-analyzer installed, and an
# unanchored pattern would match it too.
/index.scip

# Python bytecode, written beside any module a script imports. Unanchored on
# purpose, unlike /index.scip: it appears in whichever directory is imported
# from, and nothing under it is committed.
__pycache__/

# Agent-eval run artifacts. Deliberately NOT under `target/`: they are the
# evidence every number on `docs/agent-eval.md` rests on, and a `cargo clean`
# deleted four runs' worth on 2026-10-20 (#112). Not `runs/` either, which
# already holds committed recorded answers (`round*.tsv`) -- an ignored
# directory and a tracked record must not share a name.
#
# The scored outputs are COMMITTED, so a number on the page can be checked by
# anyone rather than believed: `outcomes.json` is what verifies a claim like
# "Q19 went 1/3 to 3/3", and `report.txt`, `answers.tsv` and `schema.txt` are
# what it was scored from. ~10 KB a run, and runs are rare and deliberate --
# four in two months.
#
# `agents.jsonl` is committed too (#112, decided 2026-10-26). Measured, it is
# one summary row per agent — its reply and its usage, nothing from the prompt
# or the system reminder — about 23 KB a full run, the smaller share, and it is
# the only record of per-agent cost, turns and denials that the page's cost
# and ANOMALY lines cite.
